WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:02.469 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
♪♪♪♪

00:00:02.469 --> 00:00:04.371 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Hello, everybody,
and welcome back

00:00:04.371 --> 00:00:08.408 align:start size:94% position:6%
to "Corrections"
for the week of April 19th

00:00:08.408 --> 00:00:10.810 align:start size:94% position:6%
For those of you
who do not know,

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:15.081 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Corrections"
is a digital exclusive,

00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:17.250 line:92%
which is a fancy word that means

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.120 align:start size:94% position:6%
"not good enough
for television."

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:20.787 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:00:20.787 --> 00:00:24.424 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is an ongoing
collaborative effort

00:00:24.424 --> 00:00:29.763
between me and the audienc
to get things exactly right.

00:00:29.763 --> 00:00:32.065 align:start size:94% position:6%
-[ Coughs ]
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:32.065 --> 00:00:34.968 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
I want to start last week.

00:00:34.968 --> 00:00:35.635 size:75% position:38% line:92%
This is sort of a --

00:00:35.635 --> 00:00:37.470 align:start size:94% position:6%
I should note most
of the corrections are based

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:40.774 size:88% position:44%
on things that were said
in previous corrections.

00:00:40.774 --> 00:00:42.609 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:00:42.609 --> 00:00:46.046
We talked about the fact
that Benjamin Franklin's wife --

00:00:46.046 --> 00:00:47.947
although we established,
last week, common-law wife

00:00:47.947 --> 00:00:50.383
It's really quite a story.
I got it all off Wikipedia

00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:53.887
And, while I'm reading
about Ben Franklin in Wikipedia,

00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:54.721 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
it occurred to me

00:00:54.721 --> 00:00:57.157 align:start size:94% position:6%
someone should write a boo
about that guy.

00:00:57.157 --> 00:00:59.392 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:01.561 align:start size:94% position:6%
Ben Franklin's wife's name
was Deborah

00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:03.063 line:92%
and I thought that was funny

00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:05.131 align:start size:94% position:6%
because that did not seem
like a name

00:01:05.131 --> 00:01:06.566 size:81% position:41%
that would be around
in revolutionary times

00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:08.935 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then, people pointed out
Deborah was in the Bible.

00:01:08.935 --> 00:01:10.270 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then,
I said in another corrections,

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:12.739 align:start size:94% position:6%
"What's next, Candace goin
to be in the Bible?"

00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:15.608
And then I found out Candace
was, indeed, in the Bible.

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:16.609 line:92%
And then, last week, I said,

00:01:16.609 --> 00:01:18.445 align:start size:94% position:6%
because someone
pointed out to me

00:01:18.445 --> 00:01:20.380 align:start size:94% position:6%
that Candace was
a queen of Ethiopia.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:26.252 align:start size:94% position:6%
Then I was told Candace
is a title that means "queen."

00:01:26.252 --> 00:01:29.089 line:92%
Yeah, like pharaoh, you know

00:01:29.089 --> 00:01:30.256 size:88% position:44% line:92%
So pharaoh's not a name.

00:01:30.256 --> 00:01:31.091 size:78% position:39% line:92%
It just means "king."

00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:36.096
So Candace means "queen,"
which is why, probably,

00:01:36.096 --> 00:01:40.800 align:start size:94% position:6%
one of the most popular
catchphrases in the Bible is

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.135 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
"Yasss, Candace!"

00:01:42.135 --> 00:01:47.240 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:47.974 line:92%
Did you like that, Amber?

00:01:47.974 --> 00:01:50.176 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, I did like that.
-Yeah.

00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:50.977 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:50.977 --> 00:01:55.949 align:start size:94% position:6%
I said journey man,
instead of journeyman,

00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:59.319 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then, I point outed
that a journey man

00:01:59.319 --> 00:02:00.987 size:78% position:39% line:92%
would be Steve Perry.

00:02:00.987 --> 00:02:04.757
Then, it was pointed out to me
that he's no longer in Journey

00:02:04.757 --> 00:02:07.660 size:84% position:42% line:92%
He's an ex-Journey man.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:10.063 line:92%
So I should've said Neil Schon

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:14.267 align:start size:94% position:6%
Guys, I've been getting
this one wrong

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:16.202 line:92%
for almost a full month, now

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:19.672 align:start size:94% position:6%
Prime minister of Ireland
has a title.

00:02:19.672 --> 00:02:21.040 align:start size:94% position:6%
I first said it was "Tay-sha
[Taoiseach],

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:23.843 align:start size:94% position:6%
corrected, said it last week
"Tea-sock."

00:02:23.843 --> 00:02:26.379 size:75% position:38%
I've been told, now,
it is "Tea-shock."

00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:28.148 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now, I'm sure
that's not perfect.

00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:29.249 line:92%
I also pointed out, last week,

00:02:29.249 --> 00:02:30.650 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we were really upset
to get that wrong

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:33.052 align:start size:94% position:6%
because we have
an Irish poet on staff,

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:35.188 size:88% position:44% line:92%
an old man named Seamus,

00:02:35.188 --> 00:02:36.422 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
and I pointed out

00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:38.758 align:start size:94% position:6%
that he sort of fell aslee
at the wheel on that,

00:02:38.758 --> 00:02:42.762 align:start size:94% position:6%
probably because he was
a little drunk.

00:02:42.762 --> 00:02:45.231 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I said that Seamus
often says to us

00:02:45.231 --> 00:02:47.100 line:92%
that he wants a wee taste.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.869
Someone pointed out "wee" is not
what Irish people say.

00:02:49.869 --> 00:02:51.171 line:92%
It's what Scottish people say.

00:02:51.171 --> 00:02:54.974 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, we did some digging.
We checked his paper.

00:02:54.974 --> 00:02:56.809 line:92%
It turns out Seamus is Scottish.

00:02:56.809 --> 00:02:59.279 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:02:59.279 --> 00:03:01.681 line:92%
He's an old Scottish poem.

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:02.315 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Poet, excuse me,

00:03:02.315 --> 00:03:04.884 align:start size:94% position:6%
and, obviously,
he's been let go.

00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:05.885 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:05.885 --> 00:03:06.619 size:75% position:38% line:92%
Because you don't --

00:03:06.619 --> 00:03:08.755 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, when you're lookin
to hire an Irishman,

00:03:08.755 --> 00:03:12.392 align:start size:94% position:6%
you don't want
to hire a Scots man.

00:03:12.392 --> 00:03:13.459 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:14.594 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, by the way,
we don't need an Irish poe

00:03:14.594 --> 00:03:16.896
because I realize we have a lo
of Irish guests on the show,

00:03:16.896 --> 00:03:19.799 align:start size:94% position:6%
who can teach us how
to say these words, like

00:03:19.799 --> 00:03:21.501 align:start size:94% position:6%
Sow-rees Ronan.
[Saoirse]

00:03:21.501 --> 00:03:24.337 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:24.337 --> 00:03:27.507 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was told that,
in a monologue this week,

00:03:27.507 --> 00:03:33.213 align:start size:94% position:6%
instead of a reverse mortgage,
I said "revorse" mortgage.

00:03:33.213 --> 00:03:34.948 line:92%
I found that hard to believe

00:03:34.948 --> 00:03:38.851 align:start size:94% position:6%
I did some digging
and you tell me.

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:39.586 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
A revorse mortgage.

00:03:39.586 --> 00:03:41.688 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
I mean, I heard "reverse."

00:03:41.688 --> 00:03:44.390 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:45.758 line:92%
Let's hear it one more time.

00:03:45.758 --> 00:03:48.595 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
A revorse mortgage.

00:03:48.595 --> 00:03:51.364 line:92%
You know what bothers me i

00:03:51.364 --> 00:03:52.832 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
nailed mortgage.

00:03:52.832 --> 00:03:54.367 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:54.367 --> 00:03:57.270 align:start size:94% position:6%
And there's just never
any focus on that.

00:03:58.404 --> 00:04:01.541 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:01.541 --> 00:04:04.777 align:start size:94% position:6%
I said "homonyms,"
when what I meant to say

00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:08.081 line:92%
were two words were polysemic.

00:04:08.081 --> 00:04:09.015 line:92%
I did not mean to say it.

00:04:09.015 --> 00:04:10.783 align:start size:94% position:6%
I didn't even know
that was a thing.

00:04:10.783 --> 00:04:12.518 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:12.518 --> 00:04:15.788 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Oh, we did --

00:04:15.788 --> 00:04:19.158 line:92%
We said that Jeanine Pirro

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:21.294 align:start size:94% position:6%
had a television show
on Fox News

00:04:21.294 --> 00:04:23.496 size:84% position:42% line:92%
called "Jeanine Pinot,"

00:04:23.496 --> 00:04:26.599 align:start size:94% position:6%
and a lot of people pointed ou
that, in the graphic,

00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:28.368 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
it was a red wine

00:04:28.368 --> 00:04:31.037 line:92%
and that Pinot is a white wine

00:04:31.037 --> 00:04:33.973 align:start size:94% position:6%
And a bunch of people
pointed that out

00:04:33.973 --> 00:04:36.609 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I would just like
to say to them --

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:38.678 align:start size:94% position:6%
maybe, next time
you're at a restaurant,

00:04:38.678 --> 00:04:44.751 align:start size:94% position:6%
call the sommelier over
and ask them about Pinot noir,

00:04:44.751 --> 00:04:46.085 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
you Philistines.

00:04:46.085 --> 00:04:49.822 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:49.822 --> 00:04:51.057 align:start size:94% position:6%
Also, I'm just going
to come clean

00:04:51.057 --> 00:04:54.227 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I had to go
to Shoemaker's office toda

00:04:54.227 --> 00:04:58.031 align:start size:94% position:6%
and say, "What's something
you would call people

00:04:58.031 --> 00:04:59.732 line:92%
who don't know about wine?

00:04:59.732 --> 00:05:01.534 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:03.870 line:92%
And he said, "Philistine."

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:07.540 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I used it, but I didn't
have it earlier today.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:10.209 align:start size:94% position:6%
And Philistine,
also from the Bible.

00:05:10.209 --> 00:05:13.212 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was Phyllis.
There's a woman in the Bible..

00:05:13.212 --> 00:05:16.316 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:05:16.316 --> 00:05:19.619 align:start size:94% position:6%
...an Israelite
named Phyllis Stein...

00:05:19.619 --> 00:05:21.487 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:22.689 size:75% position:38% line:92%
...who just hates --

00:05:22.689 --> 00:05:24.590 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:25.591 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
She was ve--

00:05:25.591 --> 00:05:29.028 size:88% position:44%
She was very indifferent
and hostile to the arts.

00:05:29.028 --> 00:05:30.963 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Phyllis Stein,"
they would say.

00:05:30.963 --> 00:05:33.132 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's a whole section
of it in the Bible.

00:05:33.132 --> 00:05:34.701 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:05:34.701 --> 00:05:36.536 align:start size:94% position:6%
People are like, "Phyllis,
there's a new

00:05:36.536 --> 00:05:37.503 size:84% position:42% line:92%
exhibit at the museum."

00:05:37.503 --> 00:05:39.205 align:start size:94% position:6%
She's like --
[ Mumbling ]

00:05:39.205 --> 00:05:40.973 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:05:40.973 --> 00:05:41.741 line:92%
"What do I need that for?"

00:05:41.741 --> 00:05:44.777 align:start size:94% position:6%
And they're like,
"You want some nice wine?"

00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:46.479 size:81% position:41% line:92%
And she's like, "Wine?

00:05:46.479 --> 00:05:49.248 line:92%
Who am I, the Candace of Sheba?"

00:05:49.248 --> 00:05:53.586 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:05:53.586 --> 00:05:56.622 align:start size:94% position:6%
And they would always go
to her husband, Irving Stein..

00:05:56.622 --> 00:05:57.890 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:05:57.890 --> 00:05:59.859 size:78% position:39%
...and say, "Irving,
can you talk to them?

00:05:59.859 --> 00:06:03.029
[ Laughing ]
Can you talk to Phyllis for us?"

00:06:03.029 --> 00:06:03.830 line:92%
And he would just go, "Ugh!"

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:06.432 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because he was over it.
So, a Philistine is someon

00:06:06.432 --> 00:06:09.435 align:start size:94% position:6%
who is hostile
or indifferent to the arts

00:06:09.435 --> 00:06:10.370 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
And a Irving Stein

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:14.107 align:start size:94% position:6%
is someone who is hostile
or indifferent to a Philistine

00:06:14.107 --> 00:06:16.275 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:06:16.275 --> 00:06:19.278 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Sigh ]

00:06:19.278 --> 00:06:20.546 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
So --

00:06:20.546 --> 00:06:23.216 align:start size:94% position:6%
We mentioned Pogs,
now, two weeks ago,

00:06:23.216 --> 00:06:25.952 align:start size:94% position:6%
and someone said, "Hey,
I'm calling you out, Seth.

00:06:25.952 --> 00:06:28.488 line:92%
You were 31 when Pogs came out

00:06:28.488 --> 00:06:29.288 line:92%
You didn't write that joke,"

00:06:29.288 --> 00:06:32.291 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then, last week, I said,
"I didn't. You got me.

00:06:32.291 --> 00:06:36.863
John Mulaney wrote that joke
which means, by that math,

00:06:36.863 --> 00:06:39.599 align:start size:94% position:6%
John was a 22-year-old
Pog enthusiast."

00:06:39.599 --> 00:06:41.067 align:start size:94% position:6%
Then, a bunch of people said
"Hey, man,

00:06:41.067 --> 00:06:44.437 align:start size:94% position:6%
you got bad data
from the first correctioner.

00:06:44.437 --> 00:06:47.206 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because Pogs were -- mid-'90
was the height of Pogs,"

00:06:47.206 --> 00:06:54.280 align:start size:94% position:6%
so I was 22, let's say,
when Pogs became very popular.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:55.181 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Too old for it.

00:06:55.181 --> 00:06:56.649 align:start size:94% position:6%
Mulaney was like 10
or 11 years old,

00:06:56.649 --> 00:07:00.787 align:start size:94% position:6%
so it is quite possible that h
was a Pog enthusiast then.

00:07:00.787 --> 00:07:01.788 align:start size:94% position:6%
I got confused
when I called him

00:07:01.788 --> 00:07:03.923
a Pog enthusiast last week
because, when I met him,

00:07:03.923 --> 00:07:06.826
when he was like 25, 26,
when he got hired at "SNL,

00:07:06.826 --> 00:07:08.828 line:92%
he was still super into Pogs

00:07:08.828 --> 00:07:09.629 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:07:09.629 --> 00:07:12.198 align:start size:94% position:6%
You remember.
He wanted to get paid in Pog

00:07:12.198 --> 00:07:14.567 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we had to say,
"We'll pay you money

00:07:14.567 --> 00:07:15.968 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you can use it
to buy Pogs."

00:07:15.968 --> 00:07:18.604 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he was like, "Ugh.
I'd love to

00:07:18.604 --> 00:07:20.173 size:84% position:42% line:92%
cut out the middleman."

00:07:20.173 --> 00:07:21.073 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:07:21.073 --> 00:07:23.943 line:92%
I think it was a tax thing

00:07:23.943 --> 00:07:25.611 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:07:25.611 --> 00:07:30.249 line:92%
Do you know what Pog stands for?

00:07:30.249 --> 00:07:31.684 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Anybody?

00:07:31.684 --> 00:07:34.954 align:start size:94% position:6%
Pogs were --
It's a Hawaiian thing

00:07:34.954 --> 00:07:39.659
and there was a drink called
passion-fruit orange guava

00:07:39.659 --> 00:07:41.494 line:92%
That's what Pog comes from

00:07:41.494 --> 00:07:43.930
Don't look at me like I'm
[bleep] making it up, Shoemaker.

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:45.465 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know what? That's
the hardest part about this,

00:07:45.465 --> 00:07:48.734 align:start size:94% position:6%
is how little
you want new information.

00:07:48.734 --> 00:07:50.937 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:07:50.937 --> 00:07:54.140 size:88% position:44% line:92%
You are a Phyllis Stein.

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:57.076 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:07:57.076 --> 00:07:58.711 size:75% position:38% line:92%
What else do we got?

00:07:58.711 --> 00:08:01.180 align:start size:94% position:6%
Oh, somebody said,
when we used --

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:03.649 line:92%
We used it again tonight.

00:08:03.649 --> 00:08:10.656
There's that LMFAO-Lil Jon son
"Shots, shots, shots, shots.

00:08:10.656 --> 00:08:12.959 align:start size:94% position:6%
The name of that song,
of course, is

00:08:12.959 --> 00:08:15.061 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
"Midday Revelry."

00:08:15.061 --> 00:08:16.062 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:08:16.062 --> 00:08:19.365 align:start size:94% position:6%
Somebody said, "Hey, don't pla
'Shots' that many times

00:08:19.365 --> 00:08:21.567 line:92%
without the 'Everybody.'"

00:08:21.567 --> 00:08:24.737 size:88% position:44% line:92%
That's not a correction.

00:08:24.737 --> 00:08:25.771 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
This is correct.

00:08:25.771 --> 00:08:27.707 line:92%
I'm not taking requests, here.

00:08:27.707 --> 00:08:29.408 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:08:29.408 --> 00:08:30.142 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Okay?

00:08:30.142 --> 00:08:31.978 line:92%
That's a different thing.

00:08:31.978 --> 00:08:34.146 align:start size:94% position:6%
If you want to correct me,
I'll take it,

00:08:34.146 --> 00:08:37.884 align:start size:94% position:6%
but we can't mess around
with this format.

00:08:37.884 --> 00:08:40.753 size:78% position:39%
Because nobody thinks
this is a good idea.

00:08:40.753 --> 00:08:42.822 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:08:42.822 --> 00:08:44.023 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Okay?

00:08:44.023 --> 00:08:44.790 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Nobody --

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:45.825 size:78% position:39% line:92%
I'm doing it for you.

00:08:45.825 --> 00:08:48.961
No one in my life thinks
it's good I'm doing this.

00:08:48.961 --> 00:08:50.930
My therapist was like, "Why do
you think you're doing this?

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:53.399
And I'm like, "I don't know.
Why do you think I'm doing it?

00:08:53.399 --> 00:08:55.167 align:start size:94% position:6%
And she said,
"It's obvious to me.

00:08:55.167 --> 00:08:57.737 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is due to the fact
that your father

00:08:57.737 --> 00:08:59.972 align:start size:94% position:6%
was never satisfied
with your work

00:08:59.972 --> 00:09:01.173 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you're trying
to prove to him

00:09:01.173 --> 00:09:03.643 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you can, in fact,
be perfect."

00:09:03.643 --> 00:09:05.545
And I said, "You don't kno
what you're talking about.

00:09:05.545 --> 00:09:07.246 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then, I called my dad up
and I said,

00:09:07.246 --> 00:09:09.482 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Papa, did you see
'Corrections'?"

00:09:09.482 --> 00:09:12.885 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:09:12.885 --> 00:09:15.688 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he said,
"I don't watch online content.

00:09:15.688 --> 00:09:19.892 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I said,
"It's a digital exclusive!

00:09:19.892 --> 00:09:24.030 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:09:24.030 --> 00:09:26.399 align:start size:94% position:6%
So we got to take this
seriously.

00:09:26.399 --> 00:09:27.934 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:09:27.934 --> 00:09:28.634 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Okay?

00:09:28.634 --> 00:09:31.971 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because why else
are we doing it?

00:09:31.971 --> 00:09:36.042
And I want to end tonight --
I don't want just, myself,

00:09:36.042 --> 00:09:38.377 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Tearfully ]
to reflect on the week.

00:09:38.377 --> 00:09:41.514 line:92%
I want everybody on my crew.

00:09:41.514 --> 00:09:43.449 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I'm going to leave it
to my director,

00:09:43.449 --> 00:09:45.351 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Alex, in the booth.

00:09:45.351 --> 00:09:47.019 align:start size:94% position:6%
When he feels like everyon
who works here --

00:09:47.019 --> 00:09:51.357 align:start size:94% position:6%
because this isn't just me
this is top-down.

00:09:51.357 --> 00:09:56.362
When he feels like everyon
understands the importance

00:09:56.362 --> 00:10:00.333 align:start size:94% position:6%
of getting everything right,
he can cut the feed.

